N[(1,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a sailor: wind and waves push the boat, her course changes what she meets next, and her skill and confidence decide how she sets the sails. Social cognitive theory says human functioning comes from three interacting influences: <b>personal</b> (thoughts, feelings, biology), <b>behavioural</b> and <b>environmental</b> (Bandura, 1986, 2001). They are <b>co-determinants</b>: each shapes the others, in both directions."),
("What it rejects","The theory sets itself apart from three older views. Behaviour is not only a product of outside rewards (behaviourism), not only driven by hidden inner drives (Freud), and not only free choice (many humanists). <i>In plain words:</i> you are neither a puppet of your surroundings nor completely free of them."),
("Agency","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"agency\">Human agency</b> is the ability to steer events in your life on purpose and to choose your reactions (Bandura, 2016). It works through four capacities: <b>generating new thoughts</b> (choosing helpful thoughts), <b>forethought</b> (planning, goals, imagining the future), <b>self-reaction</b> (comparing with standards and adjusting) and <b>reflective self-consciousness</b> (examining your own actions, thoughts and feelings)."),
("Self-efficacy","At the heart lies the <b>self-system</b>: how people perceive, judge and compare themselves. The most influential self-judgment is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"seff\">self-efficacy</b>, your belief that you can reach what you aim for. It is a critical determinant of self-regulation: confident learners set hard goals, monitor and correct, and persist; doubters give up easily. For Bandura (2016) it is the bedrock of aspiration, motivation and achievement."),
("The plan","The chapter covers environments and agency, the three subfunctions, the cycle (Figure 2.1), how self-regulation develops, six components, and finally research directions and practice."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("Three kinds of environment","Environments can be <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"envs\">imposed, selected or created</b> (Bandura, 1986). School is <b>imposed</b>. Within it, students <b>select</b> classes, friends and activities, and this selection may strongly shape whether they thrive or withdraw. They can also <b>create</b> their own learning environment with physical, social or technological resources, which takes a lot of agentic control. Our example: a city you did not choose, the neighbourhood you pick, the home you furnish."),
("Agency is not automatic","Having agency does not mean using it. Many people find too much self-control unpleasant and leave tasks to <b>external controls</b>, such as deadlines and reminders (Bandura, 1986, 2016). Unless students believe their own thinking and doing can improve their situation, they may not adopt high standards as their own. Self-regulation costs energy and cognitive, motivational and emotional resources."),
("So what","The environment is not just something that happens to a learner: she can choose within it and build parts of it, if she believes her actions make a difference. Next: what self-regulation is made of inside the person."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a smart scale: it shows your weight (you notice), compares it with your target (you judge), but nothing changes until you act (you react). Social cognitive theory names three <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"subf\">subfunctions</b> (Bandura, 1986): <b>self-observation</b>, <b>self-evaluation</b> and <b>self-reaction</b>."),
("Self-observation","Noticing your own thoughts and behaviour gives the information needed for change. It is coloured by your mood, memory and self-beliefs, and the more complex the task, the more attention and working memory it costs. But noticing is <b>not enough</b>: whether it leads to change depends on timing, on whether change feels <b>controllable</b> and <b>valued</b>, and on motivation. The chapter's teenager knows he games for hours, but feels no control and does not value exercise."),
("Self-evaluation","You judge yourself against <b>standards</b>. Some are external: explicit (a cut-off score) or tacit (recognition for high expectations), taught directly or picked up from models, like a girl who sees her mother expect perfection. Standards that are <b>consistent and explicit</b> are internalized most easily. People are not weathervanes: they can weigh and choose their own standards, and their personal bests become new standards. People evaluate themselves most where they care."),
("Self-reaction","You respond to your own performance. One way is a <b>tangible self-reward</b>, such as a break or a treat after finishing, which helps most when outside pressure is absent (the author, the runner). Another is <b>self-satisfaction</b>: effort and persistence go up when satisfaction depends on meeting your own standards (Bandura, 1986)."),
("So what","The three work as a chain: notice, judge, respond. When a link is missing, for example no sense of control or no valued standard, noticing alone does not reliably lead to change. The next step shows that all three work in every phase of the cycle."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The figure","<b>Figure 2.1</b> (p. 22) shows Zimmerman's cycle: <b>forethought</b> (task analysis; motivation and beliefs), <b>performance</b> (monitoring; strategy use) and <b>reflection</b> (assessment; self-reaction), with the three subfunctions in the centre. Think of a sports match: preparation, the match, the review that shapes the next preparation."),
("Forethought","Before acting, skilled learners <b>analyse the task</b> (plan, set goals, visualize) and motivate themselves. <b>Self-efficacy</b> is key: unless people believe they can do a task, they are unlikely to try it. There are kinds of efficacy: the chapter's teenager judges whether she can understand a long 19th-century novel (reading self-efficacy) and whether she can make time to read it (self-efficacy for self-regulation). <b>Goals</b> (Locke &amp; Latham, 2013), interest and outcome expectations also steer effort."),
("Performance","During the task, learners <b>monitor</b> their thinking, feelings, effort and the task's demands, and use <b>strategies</b>: note taking, help-seeking, self-talk. Athletes review recordings of their performance; marine biologists keep careful notes. Children's egocentric speech during play also serves to regulate them (Vygotsky)."),
("Reflection","Afterwards, learners look for <b>causes</b> (attributions). Blaming a changeable cause, such as \"my study approach\" for a mediocre biology grade, lets them plan differently. They review their strategies, <b>reward or sanction</b> themselves, and react emotionally: frustration makes one student try harder and another give up. Reflection can also revise self-efficacy (Schunk &amp; Usher, 2011)."),
("How it all fits","The three subfunctions work in <b>every</b> phase. Regulation covers thoughts and emotions too, and sometimes <b>not acting</b>, which can take as much effort as acting. A cycle can take minutes, days or years (Zimmerman, 2011)."),
("So what","The cycle turns the subfunctions into a timeline: think ahead and believe you can, watch yourself while doing it, then explain, react and set new goals. Next: where these skills come from."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("Four levels","Self-regulation skills are mostly <b>learned</b>. Schunk and Zimmerman (1997) describe four levels: <b>observation</b>, <b>emulation</b>, <b>self-control</b> and <b>self-regulation</b>. In the first two, the source of influence is social; in the last two, it is internal: the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"ssp\">social-self progression</b> (Schunk, 1999). Our example: watch a chef, cook along with the video, cook the recipe alone, then adapt it to what is in your fridge."),
("Observation and emulation","<b>Observation:</b> a teacher or peer models the skill and the forethought, performance and reflection behind it, and also shows her own confidence, which passes self-efficacy on vicariously. <b>Emulation:</b> students practise the modelled behaviour while the teacher gives feedback and encouragement, so their self-efficacy grows."),
("Self-control and self-regulation","<b>Self-control:</b> learners perform on their own and start to build their own efficacy beliefs, but still follow the model's pattern. <b>Self-regulation:</b> they systematically <b>adapt</b> the skill to new personal and situational conditions, start strategies themselves, evaluate their performance, and are driven by their own efficacy beliefs (Zimmerman, 2000)."),
("Not the only route","Schunk and Zimmerman did not claim this is the only way to develop self-regulation. It is a social cognitive account, and it stresses agency and self-efficacy throughout."),
("So what","Self-regulation starts outside the learner, in a model and a coach, and moves inside: watch, copy, do it alone, adapt it. Next: six components that research shows feed into self-regulation."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("Six components","Bandura and Zimmerman name six components of self-regulation: <b>cognition, behaviour, motivation, emotion, social</b> and <b>environment</b>. They act <b>together</b>: each can be the goal of an effort or a subgoal of another. Think of a car: engine, driver, driving, mood, passengers and road all affect the trip."),
("Cognition","Self-regulation needs <b>working and long-term memory</b>, and schemas that lighten the load. It depends on three <b>executive functions</b>: working memory, inhibitory control and task switching (Hofmann et al., 2012). A chaotic environment or a heavy task leaves fewer resources for monitoring. Monitoring matters most while a skill is new; once you are skilled, heavy monitoring <b>might impede</b> performance (Mace et al., 2001), which is why artists value incubation."),
("Behaviour","Visible self-regulation includes organizing your space, taking notes, recording progress, limiting distractions and rewarding yourself. <b>Klassen et al. (2008):</b> students with low self-efficacy for self-regulation procrastinated more. Students who fear failure may <b>self-handicap</b>, creating an excuse in advance; across over 25,000 students, self-handicapping went with poorer performance (Schwinger et al., 2014)."),
("Motivation","<b>Self-efficacy</b> decides whether people persist or give up. <b>Outcome expectations</b>, <b>value</b> and <b>interest</b> matter too. Mastery goals go with better strategies (Schunk &amp; Ertmer, 2000), and a purpose beyond yourself went with persistence and self-control (Yeager et al., 2014). Rewards you promise yourself have been shown to motivate more than praise or money from others (Bandura, 1986)."),
("Emotion","Emotions affect every phase, and managing them goes with better learning (Pekrun, 1992). Fear can block a start, or spur action; challenge can be a catalyst. Pride with a sense of responsibility leads to renewed effort; shame or anxiety, especially when causes feel uncontrollable, can lead to avoidance and withdrawal (Pekrun et al., 2002)."),
("Social","Regulation is socially mediated. <b>Coping models</b>, who talk about their own hard-won progress, can persuade onlookers that effort will work for them too (Pajares, 2006); a similar peer can also be motivating. Knowing when and whom to ask for help is a skill. Parents and teachers co-regulate, and the learner later uses that guidance alone. Announcing your goals creates helpful accountability."),
("Environment","Living situation, school climate and stressors all play a role. People often lean on <b>external structures</b>, such as deadlines; with heavy external control, students may get used to it, though direct instruction can build self-observation and self-evaluation. <b>Schmader et al. (2008):</b> under stereotype threat, managing the threat leaves less cognitive capacity for the task. Good self-regulators tailor their environment with timers, planners and technology, which can also distract."),
("So what","The six form a web: low self-efficacy (motivation) shows up as procrastination (behaviour); a threat (environment) costs cognitive capacity through emotion; a model (social) can rebuild belief. These links are our illustration of the chapter's point that the components interact."),
]
N[(7,0)]=[
("Open questions 1","<b>Understudied contexts:</b> most research used university students or middle-class high schools; vocational, early-childhood, distance and high-poverty settings need study. <b>High-technology environments:</b> many schools gave every student a device, but few studies with matched controls have tested the effects on self-regulation (Zheng et al., 2016)."),
("Open questions 2","<b>Feedback</b> is powerful (Hattie &amp; Timperley, 2007), but how do its forms affect the cycle? Could activity trackers or meditation assistants help learners? <b>Standards and environments:</b> people are discrepancy creators who set new goals once they reach old ones (Bandura, 2001). Why do some aim high and others settle, and how do they pick environments?"),
("Build self-efficacy","Small interventions in motivation are likely to pay off most. Teachers can build self-efficacy through <b>mastery</b> experiences, typically the strongest source (Usher &amp; Pajares, 2008), through models who overcame difficulty, and through feedback that highlights capability. They can also help students see their emotions adaptively."),
("Goals and executive function","Self-set goals and incentives motivate more than imposed ones, and internalized plans last longer. Executive functions can be trained through role play, giving children a say in their learning, and supporting their social and emotional growth (Blair &amp; Diamond, 2008). Scaffolding with suitable examples reduces cognitive load (Choi et al., 2014)."),
("Know when to stop","Effort is good, but knowing <b>when to give up</b> a goal or strategy is part of self-regulation; even growth-mindset messages can be misused (Dweck, 2015). Teachers can help students frame self-evaluation adaptively, for example by turning anger at oneself toward the task, and focus on what they can control."),
("Attention and habits","William James saw voluntary attention as the brief effort that launches new habits, and estimated that habit makes up 99.99% of daily life. Schools are ideal places to train that attention and to show students many possible ideals."),
]

