N[(1,0)][0]=("The idea","<b>Self-regulation</b> is systematically organizing your thoughts, feelings and actions to reach your goals. Think of a sailor: wind and waves push the boat, her course changes what she meets next, and her skill and confidence decide how she sets the sails. Social cognitive theory says human functioning comes from three interacting influences: <b>personal</b>, <b>behavioural</b> and <b>environmental</b> (Bandura, 1986, 2001). They are <b>co-determinants</b>: each shapes the others, in both directions.")
N[(3,0)][2]=("Self-evaluation","You judge yourself against <b>standards</b>: external ones, taught directly or picked up from models (a girl who sees her mother expect perfection), and your own. <b>Consistent, explicit</b> standards are internalized most easily; conflicting standards leave a person unsure how to act. People are not weathervanes: they can choose their standards, and personal bests become new ones. Self-set or internalized standards weigh most; without them there is little self-direction.")
N[(4,0)][0]=("The figure","<b>Figure 2.1</b> (p. 22) shows Zimmerman's cycle. <b>Forethought:</b> task analysis (planning, goal setting, visualization) and motivation and beliefs (self-efficacy, outcome expectations, implicit theory of ability, stereotype awareness). <b>Performance:</b> monitoring and strategy use. <b>Reflection:</b> assessment and self-reaction. The three subfunctions sit in the centre. Think of a sports match: preparation, the match, the review that shapes the next preparation.")
N[(4,0)][3]=("Reflection","Afterwards, learners look for <b>causes</b> (attributions). Attributing a mediocre biology grade to an <b>internal, changeable</b> cause, such as \"my study approach\", lets them plan differently. They review their strategies, <b>reward or sanction</b> themselves, and react emotionally: frustration makes one student try harder and another give up. Reflection can also revise self-efficacy (Schunk &amp; Usher, 2011).")
N[(6,0)][0]=("Six components","Bandura and Zimmerman name six components of self-regulation: <b>cognition, behaviour, motivation, emotion, social</b> and <b>environment</b>. They act <b>together</b>: each can be the goal of an effort or a subgoal of another. <i>In plain words:</i> self-regulation is not just willpower; your thinking, doing, wanting, feeling, people and surroundings all take part and affect each other.")
N[(6,0)][1]=("Cognition","Self-regulation needs <b>working and long-term memory</b>, and schemas that lighten the load. It depends on three <b>executive functions</b>: working memory, inhibitory control and task switching (Hofmann et al., 2012). A chaotic environment or a heavy task leaves fewer resources for monitoring; self-efficacy and prior knowledge cushion this. Monitoring matters most while a skill is new; once you are skilled, heavy monitoring <b>might impede</b> performance (Mace et al., 2001). So athletes avoid over-thinking, and artists value incubation (pausing monitoring).")
N[(6,0)][2]=("Behaviour","Visible self-regulation includes organizing your space, taking notes, recording progress, limiting distractions and rewarding yourself. <b>Klassen et al. (2008):</b> students with low self-efficacy for self-regulation procrastinated more. <b>Self-handicapping</b> means creating an obstacle in advance so an expected failure can be blamed on an outside cause rather than yourself (Urdan &amp; Midgley, 2001). A <b>meta-analysis</b> of over 25,000 students linked it to poorer performance (Schwinger et al., 2014).")
N[(6,0)][3]=("Motivation","<b>Self-efficacy</b> decides whether people persist or give up; outcome expectations, value and interest matter too. <b>Mastery goals</b> go with better strategies (Schunk &amp; Ertmer, 2000). <b>Performance goals</b> (beating others) make challenges discouraging and are often fuelled by believing ability is fixed (Dweck, 2006); <b>avoidance goals</b> breed defensive strategies that bring about what students fear. Self-set rewards motivate more than praise or money from others (Bandura, 1986); leaning on outside regulators lowers motivation.")
N[(6,0)][5]=("Social","Regulation is socially mediated. <b>Coping models</b>, who talk about their own hard-won progress, can persuade onlookers that effort will work for them too (Pajares, 2006); a similar peer can also be motivating. Knowing when and whom to ask for help is a skill. Parents and teachers co-regulate, and the learner later uses that guidance alone. Announcing goals creates accountability, and people choose settings for their social pull, such as a library or a gym.")
N[(6,0)][6]=("Environment","Living situation, school climate and stressors all play a role. People often lean on <b>external structures</b> such as deadlines; with heavy outside demands, students need their own regulation less and may come to depend on outside control, though direct instruction can build self-observation and self-evaluation. <b>Schmader et al. (2008):</b> under stereotype threat, managing the threat leaves less cognitive capacity for the task; strong self-efficacy can buffer. Good self-regulators tailor their environment with timers and planners.")
